# TypeScript Standards

## Strict Typing
```typescript
// ❌ BAD
const handler = async (data: any) => { ... }

// ✅ GOOD
const handler = async (data: ProfileUpdatePayload): Promise<ApiResponse<Profile>> => { ... }
```
- Never use `any`. Use `unknown` with type guards, or define a precise type.
- Always type function return values explicitly for public/exported functions.
- Prefer `interface` for object shapes; use `type` for unions, intersections, and aliases.

## Error Handling
```typescript
// ❌ BAD
} catch (e) {}

// ✅ GOOD
} catch (error) {
  const message = error instanceof Error ? error.message : 'Unknown error';
  console.error('[ServiceName] operation failed', { error, context });
  throw new ServiceError(message, { cause: error });
}
```

## Naming Conventions
| Construct | Convention | Example |
|-----------|-----------|---------|
| Types / Interfaces | PascalCase | `UserProfile`, `ApiResponse<T>` |
| Functions / Hooks | camelCase | `fetchProfile`, `useAuth` |
| Constants | SCREAMING_SNAKE | `MAX_RETRIES`, `API_BASE_URL` |
| Files (components) | PascalCase | `ProfileHeader.tsx` |
| Files (utilities) | kebab-case | `format-date.ts` |

## Utility Patterns
- Use `satisfies` for object literal type-checking without widening.
- Prefer `readonly` arrays and object properties for data flowing downward.
- Extract repeated generic patterns into utility types (`Nullable<T>`, `AsyncResult<T>`).
